WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.551
Rust projects commonly rely on crates,
build scripts, feature flags,

2
00:00:03.631 --> 00:00:06.630
procedural macros, native dependencies,
and transitive packages.

3
00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.244
These components become part of the
trusted application.

4
00:00:09.324 --> 00:00:12.272
A crate may parse input, handle network
data, manage cryptography,

5
00:00:12.352 --> 00:00:15.765
generate code, run at build time, or
connect to platform-specific libraries.

6
00:00:15.845 --> 00:00:19.258
Defensive teams review why each dependency
exists, whether it is maintained,

7
00:00:19.338 --> 00:00:21.561
which versions are allowed, which licenses
apply,

8
00:00:21.641 --> 00:00:23.710
what transitive dependencies are
introduced,

9
00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.703
and whether known security concerns exist.

10
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:27.076
The goal is not to avoid reuse.

11
00:00:27.156 --> 00:00:30.465
The goal is to understand what code is in
the product and why it belongs there.

12
00:00:30.545 --> 00:00:34.372
Feature flags deserve attention because
they can enable security-relevant behavior.

13
00:00:34.452 --> 00:00:37.141
A feature may add a parser, protocol,
cryptographic backend,

14
00:00:37.221 --> 00:00:40.065
network capability, file handling path,
platform integration,

15
00:00:40.145 --> 00:00:41.231
or optional dependency.

16
00:00:41.311 --> 00:00:44.517
Minimal feature selection can reduce the
amount of code that is compiled,

17
00:00:44.597 --> 00:00:45.528
linked, and exposed.

18
00:00:45.608 --> 00:00:49.331
Build scripts and procedural macros also
deserve review at a defensive level because

19
00:00:49.411 --> 00:00:52.721
they run as part of the build process or
generate code that becomes part of the

20
00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:53.214
program.

21
00:00:53.294 --> 00:00:56.397
Native dependencies can bring
platform-specific behavior and update

22
00:00:56.477 --> 00:00:57.356
responsibilities.

23
00:00:57.436 --> 00:00:59.246
These inputs are part of the supply chain,

24
00:00:59.326 --> 00:01:01.239
even when the final source code looks
clean.

25
00:01:01.319 --> 00:01:04.836
Lockfiles, dependency inventories,
SBOM-style records, controlled updates,

26
00:01:04.916 --> 00:01:07.294
and review gates help teams understand
what they ship.

27
00:01:07.374 --> 00:01:11.201
Updates should be tested deliberately, not
ignored indefinitely or accepted blindly.

28
00:01:11.281 --> 00:01:14.901
Defensive Rust treats dependency hygiene
as routine engineering work rather than

29
00:01:14.981 --> 00:01:15.860
emergency cleanup.
